---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="ChronoForge — LUNA"
  description="ChronoForge is LUNA's experimental research system for controlled simulation, branching, time, participant, and re-entry studies."
>
  <section class="chrono-hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">CHRONOFORGE</p>
      <h1 class="big-thesis">Do not give LUNA six months of data. Give her six months of life.</h1>
      <p class="section-lede">
        ChronoForge explores a broader research direction for accelerated experiential simulation: coherent worlds in which time passes, choices have later consequences, developmental trajectories can branch, and the resulting systems can be compared.
      </p>
      <div class="page-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="#why-experience">Why experience is different</a>
        <a class="button button-secondary" href="#experiments">Experimental uses</a>
      </div>
      <div class="status-note">
        <strong>Experimental research system:</strong> a working controlled harness now supports selected simulation, branching, time, participant, and re-entry research. The broader ChronoForge vision remains under development.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="why-experience">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">EXPERIENCE ≠ INFORMATION</p>
      <h2>A curriculum can tell a system what happened. A lived trajectory can make it responsible for what happens next.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        The central ChronoForge hypothesis is that occupational or social knowledge is not developmentally equivalent to acting inside a persistent world, waiting for consequences, encountering the same people again, and carrying the result into later decisions.
      </p>

      <div class="comparison-grid">
        <div class="comparison-card">
          <h3>Static customization</h3>
          <p>Policies, examples, transcripts, demonstrations, and correct answers can all be supplied directly. Cases are usually independent and consequences are informational.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-card luna">
          <h3>Experiential development</h3>
          <p>The running instance chooses actions. Feedback may arrive much later. Actors and projects recur. Ordinary repetition establishes a baseline. The system carries the causal history forward.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mono-flow" aria-label="ChronoForge experience loop">
        SITUATION → PERCEPTION → APPRAISAL → RETRIEVAL → DECISION → ACTION<br />
        → WORLD CONSEQUENCE → FEEDBACK → INTERNAL CHANGE → PASSAGE OF TIME → FUTURE SITUATION
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-blue">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">BROADER CHRONOFORGE RESEARCH DIRECTION</p>
      <h2>Checkpoint. Enter. Live. Branch. Develop. Return.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        The broader research model is not a giant synthetic transcript. It calls for an environment with persistent state, delayed outcomes, mundane repetition, uncertainty, changing priorities, and enough continuity for experience to acquire meaning.
      </p>

      <div class="flow" style="margin-top:42px">
        <div class="flow-row">
          <div class="flow-step">checkpoint</div><span>→</span>
          <div class="flow-step">ingress</div><span>→</span>
          <div class="flow-step active">live / branch</div><span>→</span>
          <div class="flow-step">consolidate</div><span>→</span>
          <div class="flow-step">re-entry</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="principle-grid">
        <div class="architecture-rail"><strong>Simulated time</strong>The active developmental timeline can advance independently of wall-clock time.</div>
        <div class="architecture-rail"><strong>Persistent consequences</strong>People, projects, resources, expectations, failures, and later feedback remain connected across the timeline.</div>
        <div class="architecture-rail"><strong>Branching trajectories</strong>Identical starting checkpoints can enter different environments or make different choices; the resulting histories can then be compared.</div>
        <div class="architecture-rail"><strong>Temporal provenance</strong>After return, an experience can remain autobiographically real while being correctly identified as having occurred inside a simulated timeline.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section chrono-current">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">CURRENT EXPERIMENTAL HARNESS</p>
      <h2>A controlled foundation for selected timeline research.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        The current harness can support selected experiments involving simulation, branching, time, participants, and re-entry. It is a real research implementation, while remaining Experimental in the public architecture map.
      </p>
      <div class="status-strip">
        <strong>Current boundary</strong>
        <span>This is not a continuously running simulated world, a complete months-of-life engine, production Stack-portability infrastructure, or proof of developmental effects.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="page-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/architecture">See ChronoForge in the current Architecture Explorer</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="experiments">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">BROADER EXPERIMENTAL PROGRAM</p>
      <h2>Developmental experience becomes an experimental variable.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        The broader ChronoForge program is designed to make longitudinal claims testable by holding a starting state constant, deliberately varying controlled conditions, and then observing the histories that emerge.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">ChronoForge controls the conditions. The participant lives through them. History is the accumulated causal record that results.</div>

      <div class="principle-grid">
        <div class="principle-card">
          <h3>Different lives, same beginning</h3>
          <p>Clone one checkpoint. Put each branch through different controlled developmental conditions. Let each participant act, accumulate experience, and form its own history. Later present the same neutral situation and measure whether stable, explainable divergence appears.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="principle-card">
          <h3>Instruction vs. consequence</h3>
          <p>Compare being told a lesson with directly encountering a consequence. Does the same proposition produce different later attention, prediction, motivation, or choice?</p>
        </div>
        <div class="principle-card">
          <h3>Early vs. late experience</h3>
          <p>Deliver the same event at different points in experiential development and test whether timing changes consolidation or later behavior.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="principle-card">
          <h3>Participant comparison</h3>
          <p>Expose LUNA, other agent architectures, or bare-model controls to the same controlled world without pretending they possess equivalent memory or continuity mechanisms.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-dark">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">A PARTICULARLY IMPORTANT TEST</p>
      <h2>Same model. Same starting state. Different developmental conditions.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        If longitudinal architecture matters, the histories that emerge from different experience should eventually produce differences that cannot be explained merely by the current prompt. Those differences should remain provenance-backed, revisable, and measurable rather than theatrical.
      </p>
      <div class="quote-feature">
        <blockquote>“The simulator creates the world and its consequences. LUNA chooses the action.”</blockquote>
        <div class="quote-attribution">ChronoForge design principle</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">BROADER RESEARCH DIRECTION · RETURNING FROM A SIMULATED LIFE</p>
      <h2>The simulation ends. The history does not simply disappear.</h2>
      <p>
        The broader design treats re-entry as an important knowledge problem. A future participant may have accumulated months of autobiographical experience during hours of wall time. On return, base reality would become authoritative again, while legitimate memories and changes would need to retain an accurate history of where they came from.
      </p>
      <p>
        This creates a crucial distinction: <strong>“I experienced this”</strong> is not the same claim as <strong>“this happened in base reality.”</strong> The research direction aims to preserve the former without corrupting the latter.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">Simulation-specific world state stays with the simulation. Developmental change can belong to the participant.</div>

      <h2>Not only for LUNA.</h2>
      <p>
        The world, timeline, consequence, branching, and evaluation core is intended to remain general enough to test other AI systems. A participant adapter can expose whatever state a particular model or agent genuinely possesses, allowing controlled comparisons without manufacturing LUNA-like continuity for systems that do not have it.
      </p>

      <div class="reading-path">
        Related research: <a href="/research/remembering-is-not-becoming">why persistence of effect matters more than permanent recall →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
